# Release

How a commit gets measured, how the measurement reaches the forge, and how a
measured `dev` becomes a tagged, published, announced `main`. One document
because it is one mechanism seen at three moments: at every push, at the merge,
and on the badge a reader lands on afterwards.

## What a release is

A release is a merge of `dev` into `main`, and every such merge is one. Four
things travel together and a release is half done until all four exist: the
merge commit on `main`, an annotated tag on that commit named for the version,
a GitHub release on that tag whose body is the changelog block, and the registry
publish of the version the manifest names. Nothing is released off `dev`, and a
release tag on any commit `main` cannot reach is a defect the check below names.

The one carve-out is a hotpatch or a small thing that had to reach `main` and
was not worth a version. It is merged without the other three, it is rare, and
it is said to be that when it happens. The next release then carries it.

`0.x` versions bump the way cargo and jsr read them: a breaking change on a
`0.x` line is a minor. The version itself is never inferred; the level is given.

## The gate

The gate is a fixed list of steps run against one commit of one repo, on the
machine of whoever is pushing, and it produces a verdict and a set of numbers.
It decides what a repo is from the marker files at its root, and which files
are markers is the workspace's to say: `[markers]` in `homma.toml` maps a file
name to what it signals, one of `cargo`, `deno` or `content`. `Cargo.toml`
signalling `cargo` and `deno.json` signalling `deno` are the two rows every
workspace has without writing them; a workspace adds rows for the files its
other repositories carry, `polka.toml` or `mockspace.toml` say, and a signal
outside those three words is refused when the manifest loads, naming the row.
A repo's kind is the set of signals its root carries: `cargo` adds the cargo
calls to a step, `deno` the deno calls, and `content` adds nothing, so a
repository whose markers say only `content` runs a gate of skipped steps, bar
notices, and passes. A root carrying no declared marker at all is an error,
since that is a repository homma has not been told about, which is a different
thing from one it has been told holds only content. A repo that keeps its
manifests under a subdirectory, `mock/` in this workspace, is read as whatever
its root markers say, `content` where that is all `mockspace.toml` is declared
to mean; the workspace-wide run names such a repo as passed over rather than
skipping it in silence, and reading the manifest under `mock/` for its real
kind is owed. What a step runs per signal:

| step | `cargo` | `deno` | blocks |
|---|---|---|---|
| format | `cargo fmt --check` | `deno fmt --check` | yes |
| lint | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, with all features, or once per declared feature set | `deno lint` then `deno check` on every export | yes |
| tests | `cargo test` with all features and with none, or once per declared feature set | `deno test` with the permissions `deno.json` declares | yes |
| deny | `cargo deny check`, only when `deny.toml` exists | nothing | yes |
| docs | rustdoc coverage on nightly, the documented fraction of public items, with all features or on the declared sets | `deno doc --lint` | reports |
| notices | `ante check`, only when `ante.toml` exists | the same | yes |
| description | every published `Cargo.toml` description against its readme's tagline | every named `deno.json` description against the same | yes |

The description step is the one that runs no program: it reads the checkout
and compares two lines of it. The tagline is the readme's blockquote under
the title, the first line beginning `>` after the first heading, wherever
the badge block puts it and inside a `<div>` or not, with the marker stripped
and a quote wrapped over two lines joined by a space; it is the line a
stranger reads first on the forge, and the opening prose after the badge
block is a different element and is never taken for it. A title written
setext style, underlined rather than hashed, is a title. The description is
what the same stranger reads first on the registry, and the manifests read
are the ones that reach one: every `Cargo.toml` in the tree whose `[package]`
does not say `publish = false`, and every `deno.json` carrying a `name`,
`target/` and `node_modules/` left unwalked. Each is held to the tagline of
the readme beside it where one exists, and to the root readme's otherwise,
so a launcher crate under a virtual root is held to the root readme, a
workspace publishing several crates with a readme each is held crate by
crate, and an engine crate nobody publishes is not read at all. The two are
compared with the whitespace at their ends trimmed and nothing else, since a
trailing full stop one carries and the other lacks is the drift being
caught, and a manifest that declares no description fails the same way, a
registry line that says nothing being the same absence as one that says
something else. A readme with no blockquote under its title has no tagline,
and a manifest measured against one is a skip that says so: the gate cannot
compare against nothing, and the readme's own shape is another gate's
business. A tree with no published manifest is a plain skip. The log names
both strings where they differ, and the manifest each pair belongs to.

A step that blocks turns the verdict red on failure. A step that reports puts its
number in the record and the verdict stands. The numbers a run produces are the
test count and how many passed, the documented fraction, the deny advisory count
where it ran, and the wall time of the whole gate; anything a step prints is kept
in the record as its log rather than summarised. The run's one-line summary,
which is also the description on the commit status, names the steps that ran
and how each went; a run in which every step was skipped, which is what a
repository whose markers say only content produces, summarises as nothing to
run and says why, so a green status on such a repository never reads as a
check that happened.

A crate declares the feature combinations it builds under as
`[package.metadata.homma] feature_sets = [["a"], ["a", "b"]]` in its manifest,
and where it does, those sets are the whole of what the gate builds for it:
lint, tests and docs each run once per set, `--no-default-features` with that
set enabled, and `--all-features` is never run for it, since a crate whose
features exclude each other has said which builds are legal and the gate does
not build an illegal one. An empty declaration is refused as a manifest error,
since it would gate nothing and pass. A crate without the entry is built with
all features and with none. On a virtual workspace root, the workspace-wide
runs exclude every member declaring sets, so a plain member keeps its two runs
and a declaring member gets its own against itself; a root that is itself a
package builds alone as it always did, and a declaring member under it gets
its own runs beside that. The docs number is read off the first set's coverage
table. A gate never edits a tree; it runs in the checkout it is given and
fails if that checkout is dirty, because a number measured on a tree with
unstaged changes describes no commit.

## The run record

A gate run lands in the store as a record of kind `gate-run`, which the
workspace declares like any other kind, append-only. Its attributes: `repo` as a
reference into the repos namespace, `sha` as text, `ran_at` as an instant,
`verdict` as text holding `green` or `red`, `steps` as text holding the per-step
outcome and numbers as json, and `log` as text. A number rides as text because
the store's three attribute types are what the records that exist have needed,
and a fourth is added when a kind needs to sort or sum one, which this does not.

The record is what the release reads. `homma release run` looks for the newest
`gate-run` on the exact sha at the tip of `dev`, refuses when there is none or
the newest is red, and never runs the gate itself. So the gate runs once, at the
push, and everything after it trusts the record. A run against a different sha,
however recent, is not evidence about this one.

## The commit status

Every run posts a commit status to the forge on the sha it measured, under the
context `homma/gate`, with `success` or `failure` as the state and the numbers
in the description in the order the table lists them. The forge api is the same
shape on GitHub and Forgejo, one write per status, and the forge trait grows one
method for it. The token comes from the forge's configured `token_cmd`.

The trunk rulesets on `dev` and `main` require `homma/gate` to be `success` on
the head of any pull request before it can merge, and the workspace's ruleset
applicator carries that requirement. Nothing runs on the remote. The remote is
told that something ran here, and it refuses a merge until it has been told,
which is the whole of how a locally run gate governs a remotely merged branch.

A status write that fails because the network is down does not turn the run red.
The record is kept and the status is posted by the next run or by
`homma release gate --post <sha>`, and until then the forge holds the merge,
which is the correct state for a commit the forge has not heard about.

## Hooks

Every git hook homma has a hand in is one entrypoint per event, written by
`homma hook install <repo>` into the repository's own git directory under
`hooks/`, and what each entrypoint runs is a table in `homma.toml`. The
entrypoint is a few lines that call `homma hook run <event>` with git's own
arguments, so the logic stays in the binary and the file never drifts from it.

`[hooks]` holds one array of entries per git event, `[[hooks.pre-commit]]`,
`[[hooks.pre-push]]` and so on, each entry a `run` command and, where it
should run only for some paths, `paths`, an array of globs. The event names
are git's own, and a name git has no hook for is refused when the manifest
loads, naming the row, the way a bad marker signal is. One row every workspace
has without writing it: the release gate, `homma release gate --hook`, on
`pre-push`, first, so a workspace that writes no table pushes gated and one
that writes a table cannot drop the gate by omission. Declared entries follow
it in the order written.

`homma hook run <event> [args...]` captures stdin once, for the events git
hands one to, `pre-push`, `pre-receive`, `post-receive`, `post-rewrite`,
`reference-transaction` and `proc-receive`, and reads none for the rest,
since a hook handed an open pipe by whatever wraps git would otherwise wait
on it; it works out what the event touches, then runs the entries whose
globs match, in order, through the shell with stdin replayed to each, and
stops at the first non-zero exit, which is its own. What an event touches:
the staged paths for `pre-commit` and `commit-msg`; for `pre-push`, the union
over every pushed ref of what its local tip adds over its remote, widened to
everything the tip carries where the remote is a new branch or a commit this
clone does not have; nothing for any other event, so an entry with globs
there never runs and one without runs as always. An entry
without `paths` runs on every invocation. An entry with `paths` runs when at
least one touched path matches one of its globs, and `{paths}` in its `run`
expands to those matching paths, quoted, so a checker that takes files is
handed exactly the files that made it run. What git handed the hook after its
own name, the message file on `commit-msg`, the remote's name and url on
`pre-push`, reaches an entry through `{args}` and only through it: appended
to every command they would land on a checker that takes files as a file, and
on a command that takes none as an error. Each entry gets one line naming it
and how it went, and an event with no entries is a pass that says so.

The gate entry gates every tip being pushed and refuses the push when the
verdict is red. A green run writes its record and posts its status before the
push proceeds, so a branch never reaches the remote without its status.

The install writes into git's own hooks directory, `.git/hooks` or the
worktree's equivalent, whatever `core.hooksPath` says, because that is the one
place every hook chain reaches: git reads it directly where the path is unset,
and mockspace's durable gate runs the repository's own hook there after its
own checks, with the same arguments and stdin, where the hook git will run
for that event is mockspace's. That is decided per event and off the file:
what sits at `<core.hooksPath>/<event>` and says in its first lines that it is
mockspace's, since mockspace writes its gate for three events and a path that
merely spells its name says nothing about the others. The install says, for
each entrypoint it wrote, which of those holds, and where nothing under the
path reaches it, no file there, or some other tool's, it says git will not
run the entrypoint until that tool chains to it; one such event makes the
exit non-zero, with the lines saying which. It refuses one thing, reported
the same way so a sweep across a workspace goes on to the next repo: a hook
already there for that event that is not homma's, whatever it holds and
whether or not it can be read, since it is somebody's work. No refusal leaves
a partial install, since every event is checked before any is written, and
homma's own entrypoints are rewritten in place. A hooks directory the
repository tracks was a refusal while the install wrote wherever
`core.hooksPath` pointed; git's own directory is nothing a repository can
track, so it is not one now.

## The check

Before anything moves, `homma release check <repo>` establishes the invariants
and prints every one it cannot, by id. The set a release needs green is this:

The tags: every release tag is reachable from `main` (`tag.reachable`, tightened
to `main`), on the remote and pointing at the same commit both sides
(`tag.pushed`, `tag.sha.agrees`), and every version bump commit on `main`
carries one (`tag.bump.tagged`). That a tag is annotated (`tag.annotated`),
named `vX.Y.Z` or `X.Y.Z` consistently within the repo (`tag.prefix`), alone on
its commit (`tag.dupes`) and fetched (`tag.local`) is hygiene, reported at warn.

`main` holds nothing unreleased: the newest release tag is the tip of `main`, or
what lies between is a hotpatch named as such (`main.unreleased`, the one id
the catalogue did not have, since it never read `main`). The manifest version at a tag
equals the tag (`man.version.matches`), and the working version is above the
highest published one and its smallest legal successor at the level given
(`man.current.forward`, `man.current.smallest`).

The registry: published versions ascend in publish order (`order.ascends`),
skip no number (`semver.gaps`), every published version has a tag
(`reg.orphan`), the newest tag has a published version (`reg.unpublished`,
which blocks on the newest tag and warns on older ones, since a tag from before
the package was ever published is history and not a release nobody finished),
and where a package ships on both jsr and npm the two hold the same set
(`both.sameset`).

The tree: clean, on a branch, nothing unpushed, no untracked file
(`tree.clean`, `tree.attached`, `tree.pushed`, `tree.untracked`), and no
workflow file anywhere in the tree (`hist.workflow.tree`).

The rest of the catalogue, the bundle contents, the history scans, the branch
hygiene and the reader-facing checks, is reported at `warn` and does not block,
and each carries the same id it had before.

## The run

`homma release run <repo> --level <patch|minor|major>` is the release, and the
level is required: a run without one refuses, and the tool never reads a level
off the commits. What it does, in order, stopping at the first refusal:

1. Runs the check and refuses on any blocking finding.
2. Finds the newest green `gate-run` for the tip of `dev` and refuses without it.
3. Prints the plan: the two branches it carries, `dev` onto `main`, or `main`
   alone where the repo has no working branch here or on the remote; the
   commits since the last release tag with their prefixes,
   the version the level makes of the last tag's, the changelog block those
   commits become, and what publishes where, in dependency order. A manifest
   already at that version is the release and nothing bumps; a manifest at any
   other version above the last tag disagrees with the level, and the plan
   refuses with both numbers rather than picking one. With `--dry-run` it stops
   here.
4. Bumps the manifest on `dev`, prepends the block to `CHANGELOG.md`, commits
   both as one commit, and pushes `dev`.
5. Merges `dev` into `main` with a merge commit, never a squash, never a
   fast-forward, so the tag has a commit of its own and `main` keeps every
   constituent commit.
6. Creates the annotated tag on the merge commit and pushes it.
7. Creates the GitHub release on the tag with the changelog block as its body.
8. Publishes to every registry the manifest names, in dependency order across
   the repos the plan named, waiting for each registry to serve a version before
   the next package that depends on it publishes.
9. Writes the badge json to the `badges` branch.

A cargo workspace publishes its members in dependency order, one `cargo publish`
per member. A deno package publishes with `deno publish`, and where it also ships
on npm, builds the npm package and publishes that too. The registry tokens come
from the same credential tool the forge token does.

`homma release run` without a repo releases every repo the workspace names that
has something unreleased, with one `--level` for all of them, which is the
shape a docs pass across the estate takes. The order is by name today, and a
run whose set carries a dependency edge, one repo it would release naming
another as a git or registry dependency, is refused with the two names, so the
dependent is released after its dependency by two runs by name. Dependency
order across repos is owed, the way the publish already orders the members of
one workspace.

## The changelog

`CHANGELOG.md` lives on `main` in every released repo, newest first, one block
per release, kept forever. A block opens with the version and the date and
carries the commits since the previous tag grouped by their conventional prefix,
`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`, in that order, one line each
in the commit's own words with its short sha, and the pull request number where
the merge commit carries one. Commits with no prefix land under `other`. The
same block, verbatim, is the GitHub release body. There is no summary paragraph
and no prose the tool writes on its own.

## Badges

Each repo carries an orphan branch named `badges` holding one json file per
measured thing, in the shape shields' endpoint badge reads: a schema version, a
label, a message and a colour. The files: `tests.json`, `docs.json`,
`deny.json` where deny ran, and `gate.json` for the verdict, plus `version.json`
naming the version that shipped. The release rewrites the branch on every run,
so a badge shows the number the gate measured for the commit the tag points at.

A readme references one as
`https://img.shields.io/endpoint?url=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/<repo>/badges/<file>`.
The branch is fetched by every clone, a few kilobytes and never checked out,
and that is accepted over a ref outside `refs/heads/` that raw file hosting
cannot resolve.

## Credentials

The forge token comes through the forge's `token_cmd` or `token_env`, as every
forge call already does. The registry tokens come through the same command with
the registry as its argument (`crates-io`, `jsr`, `npm`), and the publish steps
hand them to `cargo publish`, `deno publish --token` and an npm user config
written for the call, never to the environment of the shell that ran homma.

## The surface

```
homma release check [<repo>]              the invariants, by id, blocking ones first
homma release gate [<repo>] [--sha <sha>] run the gate now, record it, post the status
homma release gate --hook                 what the pre-push entry runs
homma release gate --post <sha>           post a recorded run's status again
homma release plan [<repo>] --level <l>   the plan and nothing else
homma release run [<repo>] --level <l>    the release, or every unreleased repo
homma release badges <repo>               rewrite the badges branch from the newest record
homma hook install <repo>                 write one entrypoint per event, or refuse and say why
homma hook run <event> [args...]          what an entrypoint calls: the event's entries, in order
```

Every subcommand prints for a human when a terminal is attached and its result
alone when one is not, the split the root design decides for the whole surface.
